---
id: org.dxos.plugin.projects
name: ProjectsPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

Projects: long-running work composed of instructions, skills, tasks, routines, artifacts and chats in the
project's context. A project starts from a template (`src/templates/`); this document covers the templates
whose run is a demo worth walking end to end.

The **Composer Plugin** template (`src/templates/composer-plugin.ts`) is offered in the desktop app: an agent
builds a TypeScript Composer plugin in a local sandbox and offers it back to load. The sandbox holds nothing but
the bun runtime the app ships; the guide comes from GitHub and the packages from pkg.pr.new and npm, pinned to
the commit and library versions the app was built from (`VITE_DX_PLUGIN_TOOLCHAIN`, stamped by composer-app's
vite config). The browser has no local sandbox, so there plugin-dev-shell contributes its own version of the
template, which builds with the dev shell of a vite server.

## Extensions

| Term       | URI                         |
|------------|-----------------------------|
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`     |
| `test`     | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`     |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0` |

## Features

```mdl
feat F-4: A project's specs
  req F-4.1: A project owns any number of top-level specs (`Project.specs`), and each spec has at most one owner. A
             plugin may make an object of its own type a spec's owner, such as a feature its spec breaks down.
  req F-4.2: A spec (`@dxos/compute`'s `Spec`) holds a name, a description, its dialects and its document. The document
             is the spec; any other view is a lens over it. A spec is part of its project, not a type of its own in a
             space, so Create object does not offer it. Its document holds no front matter: the name is the spec's,
             edited in the nav tree or the overview.
  req F-4.3: A spec's dialects name the dialect specs whose blocks its document uses, as data on the spec, not in its
             text. A spec with none uses the core and changelog blocks only.
  req F-4.4: What a dialect reads out of a document is kept as typed annotations on the spec, defined by the plugin
             that contributes the dialect.
  req F-4.5: The project article's Specs tab shows one spec, picked from a dropdown in its toolbar that also offers Add
             spec. It opens on the spec picked last, or the project's first.
  req F-4.6: The nav tree lists the project's specs under its Specs branch at `project/<id>/specs/<spec>`, and a spec a
             project owns opens as the project's article on that spec. A spec that a `Ref<spec>` field of another
             spec's block cites, by object id (`echo:///<id>`) or spec id, sits beneath each spec citing it at
             `…/specs/<spec>/<cited>`, not at the top; a cycle stops before a spec repeats, and a spec that only a cycle
             reaches is listed at the top. Another plugin nests levels of its own beneath a
             spec with `ProjectGraph.makeLevelNode`, at `…/specs/<spec>/<level>/…`, each node holding its object. A
             level opens as the project's article narrowed to it, through the `ProjectSurface.Level` surface; its
             Overview tab is the nesting plugin's `ProjectSurface.LevelOverview` surface, and its Specs tab shows the
             level's own spec, or offers Add spec when it has none.
  req F-4.7: The project article's toolbar holds its tabs, then the tools of the tab shown: the spec dropdown and
             the Extensions menu on the Specs tab; Delegate to agent and the Gantt toggle on the Tasks
             tab. The Overview has no tools there. It has no overflow menu and no button to start a session, which is
             the assistant companion's.
  req F-4.8: The Specs tab shows the spec's text. The spec's other views are in its companion tabs, Structure,
             Decisions and Review, beside the spec and beside a project with specs: each view a plugin contributes
             (`ProjectCapabilities.SpecView`) to a tab's group for dialects the spec uses, drawn by that plugin's
             `ProjectSurface.SpecView` surface. A tab with no view for the spec's dialects is not offered; beside a
             project, the spec is the one its Specs tab shows. A tab shows a toggle between its views when it has more than one, and
             the toolbar a plugin draws through `ProjectSurface.SpecToolbar`. A package is a set of dialects a
             plugin contributes (`ProjectCapabilities.SpecPackage`). The Extensions menu lists the packages and checks each one whose dialects the spec all uses;
             checking one adds its dialects and unchecking removes them.
  req F-4.10: A spec's text offers a slash command for each block kind its dialects and its own `ext` blocks define. A
              command writes the block in place, fenced as `mdl`, with a fresh key as the id of a numbered kind and
              its required fields empty; text before the slash becomes the block's title, and the block's form takes
              the focus.
  req F-4.11: The project's overview is one form over the `Project` schema, in which the specs are a list the project
              owns: each spec is a row folded under its name that unfolds to edit its name and description in place,
              and the list's `+` creates a spec, its row open for the name.
  req F-4.12: A spec's text draws object links and embeds as a Markdown document does, and takes the editor extensions
              other plugins give Markdown documents (`MarkdownCapabilities.ExtensionProvider`), such as diagrams. Its
              packages add their own editing on top.
  req F-4.13: A spec's text, and each block form drawn in it, shows the carets of the other peers editing it, each
              with the peer's name.
  req F-4.14: A spec's text opens in preview, where each block of a kind its schemas define is drawn as a form over that
              kind's fields, and offers readonly and source views. The text is the only store: a form edit changes only
              the source of the field it edits, and the title, the prose and each prose field are editors of their own
              over their source, showing the other peers' carets and the comments anchored in them. A form takes about
              the room of its source: a header row with the block's label, its title and its choice fields as tags, then
              a row per other field with its name small before its value, and a line per keyed entry and per map row.
              Empty fields and the add and remove actions show only while the form has focus, a form folds to its header
              row, and prose it cannot place shows as a note pointing to the source view.
  req F-4.15: An agent reads and writes a spec's blocks with `GetSpecBlocks`, `SetSpecBlock` and `DeleteSpecBlock`,
              naming a block by key or positional label. A write is checked against the kind's schema and changes only
              the parts it names; a new block of a numbered kind, and each new keyed entry, gets a fresh key.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: A project's overview
  req F-5.1: The overview opens on the project's name as its title, its status as a tag in the top right corner and
             its description below, each edited in place with no edit button. The name and description start at the
             same left edge as the section headings. The tag shows the status's capitalized name and its icon in its colour.
  req F-5.2: Below the header the sections are, in order: Notes, Artifacts, Milestones, Specs and Config, with more
             space between two sections than between the rows inside one. Every section's content starts and ends on
             the same left and right edges as its heading.
  req F-5.3: Artifacts shows at most six, and Show all shows the rest. Its heading carries the add button, as the
             Specs heading does.
  req F-5.4: Each milestone shows how many of its tasks are done out of those not cancelled, and opens the Tasks tab
             filtered to the tasks under it.
  req F-5.7: The Specs heading carries the add button on its own row, and each spec is a row directly under it at the
             same level of nesting as a Config group.
  req F-5.8: An empty Notes invites notes, plans and next steps where they are typed, and an empty Artifacts or Specs
             says the project has none yet.
  req F-5.5: Config holds folded groups, each showing a summary of what it holds until it is opened: first Agent (the
             instructions, skills and context), then the groups other plugins declare.
  req F-5.6: A plugin declares a group with `ProjectCapabilities.ConfigGroup`: its fields are each a schema and a
             writable atom holding the project's value, which the overview's form renders and writes with no UI of the
             plugin's own. Groups with the same id merge, their fields sorted by each field's order, and a field
             whose `enabled` atom is false is hidden. A field may carry an action, a button at the end of its control
             that sets its value.
  req F-5.9: The project's name and description, the overview's Notes and the agent's instructions show the carets of
             the other peers editing them, each with the peer's name.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Definitions
  req F-6.1: Definitions groups a spec by what names its blocks: each anchor is a group of the structure it names, and
             groups gather into clusters around the block most of them share.
  req F-6.2: Definitions draws the whole spec as a canvas, with no layout of its own to pick.
  req F-6.3: The canvas opens a level at a time: a card per cluster, then one cluster's groups, then one block and
             everything it touches. Double-clicking a card opens its level, and the canvas's trail goes back up.
  req F-6.4: Every block draws with the icon and colour its dialect declares, and every link with the ends of the
             relationship its field declares. A package may style a field's values on the cards (`SpecPackage.facts`).
  req F-6.5: Related, Structure's second view, centres on the block nearest the editor's cursor, with what names it
             above and what it names below, and moves to another block as the cursor does.
  req F-6.6: A spec's blocks are read with the dialects it names and no others; a spec that names none uses the core
             and changelog blocks only.
  req F-6.7: The companion views read the spec's text. A plugin that knows where a spec comes from gives them the rest
             through `ProjectCapabilities.SpecSourcesProvider`: the base text diff mode compares with, the specs its
             source keys name, the source index, and its files.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Decisions
  desc: |
    Decisions mirrors Structure: At cursor reads the changelog for the block at the editor's cursor, and the Ledger
    reads all of it, a level at a time. Both are built from react-ui's existing components.
  req F-7.1: At cursor, Decisions' first and default view, shows what holds on the item at the editor's cursor, each
             with the decisions that fill it in, then what holds on its block or on the block's items, and moves as
             the cursor does.
  req F-7.2: The Ledger opens on a card per block the changelog affects, the most recently decided first. A block
             opens its threads, the decisions on it that cite one another; a thread opens with what holds now, then
             its decisions by day. The trail goes back up.
  req F-7.3: The Ledger filters by status (holds, replaced, not approved, questions), by who decided, and by text.
  req F-7.4: Every level offers to approve the agent decisions in it that hold and that nobody has approved, with
             the count on the button. On a thread it sits at the top. Approving records one decision by the person,
             `Approve [D-a] and [D-b].`
  req F-7.5: A decision shows how it relates to each decision it cites (fills in, replaces, replaces part of,
             refines, builds on), read from the prose before the citation.
  req F-7.6: An answer to a question, or an approval, goes through the spec's open editor, else into its text.
  req F-7.7: A decision's chip in the editor's margin opens the Ledger at the decision's thread.
```

```mdl
feat F-8: Review
  req F-8.1: Review is offered for a spec of any dialect and shows its `SpecReview`s.
  req F-8.2: The Review view picks a review from the spec's history; it shows the current status by default.
  req F-8.3: The Review view shows the review's summary as markdown: its first paragraph, with the rest behind a
             disclosure. Below it, a filter of each status with its count, worst first, stays pinned above the cards
             and shows only that status's verdicts; each status is a button of its own, its icon beside its count.
             The summary, filter and cards scroll together under the picker, and the current card shows its note in
             full.
```

## QA

Executable tests. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps to an invocable
operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by test and step number.

```mdl
test QA-1: Build a plugin with an agent in the desktop app
  desc: |
    An agent works the Composer Plugin template's parent task and four subtasks in a local sandbox — fetch the
    guide, write a TypeScript plugin, install and build it, publish and offer it — and the reader loads the
    result. The plugin adds a "World Clock" group to each space's navtree with a "Clocks" page under it: a world
    map from plugin-map above a row of live clocks, kept in a `Clock` ECHO object. Walked by the autocue flow
    `packages/plugins/plugin-projects/autocue/composer-plugin-desktop.mjs` through `driver.mjs --target tauri`.
  actors: both
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open (the default "My Space" is fine)
    - Composer is the desktop app, built from a commit of `main` that pkg.pr.new has published (a deployed
      build's commit is), or with `DX_PLUGIN_TOOLCHAIN_COMMIT` naming one
    - Sandbox is enabled and its Backend is Local, the desktop app's default
    - no plugin named "World Clock" is loaded, and no "World Clock" project or sandbox is left from an earlier
      run (the flow's first setup step restores this)
  steps:
    - name: Create a project from the Composer Plugin template
      do: Click + on the space, choose Project, pick the "Composer Plugin" template, title it "World Clock" and click Create.
      capture: project
      expect: a "World Clock" project opens; its Tasks tab holds "Build the World Clock plugin" over four subtasks
    - name: Delegate the parent task to the agent
      do: Open the project's Tasks tab, tick the parent task, click "Delegate selected tasks to agent", then open the companion's Assistant tab.
      expect: the delegated chat holds the parent and its four subtasks and is working
    - name: Wait for the agent to offer the plugin
      do: Watch the subtasks tick off.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.queryObjects { typename: "org.dxos.type.sandbox", text: "World Clock" }
      capture: sandboxes
      expect: |
        a "World Clock" sandbox exists, all five tasks are in `review`, and the chat shows a "Load plugin" prompt whose
        URL is `http://localhost:<port>/files/<key>/manifest.json`
      assert: return $result.results.length === 1
      note: |
        Every command the agent runs is in that sandbox: `curl` fetches the guide, `bunx @pnpm/exe` installs,
        `bun run --bun` typechecks and builds. Nothing is read from a source tree on this machine.
    - name: Load and enable the plugin
      do: Click Load on the prompt, then open Plugins, then Labs, and turn World Clock on.
      expect: World Clock loads from the sandbox's published directory and activates, with plugin-map
    - name: Open the Clocks page and add two timezones
      do: Click Clocks under the WORLD CLOCK group, then add two timezones with the plus card.
      expect: the page shows the map above three ticking clock cards and the plus card
  after:
    - name: Delete the project
      do: Right-click the "World Clock" project in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [$project.id], target: $given.space }
      expect: the project leaves the navtree
    - name: Delete the sandbox
      do: Right-click the "World Clock" sandbox in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { refs: [{ "/": $sandboxes.results[0].dxn }], target: $given.space }
      expect: the sandbox leaves the navtree and its shell environment is torn down
    - name: Uninstall World Clock
      do: Open Plugins, then Labs, open the World Clock card's detail page and click Uninstall.
      expect: World Clock leaves the registry and its WORLD CLOCK group leaves the navtree
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Build a plugin with an agent in the browser and install it from the private registry
  desc: |
    The browser variant of QA-1. The agent works the same parent task and four subtasks in a Sandbox on EDGE, and the
    last one publishes the build with `dx registry publish --private`, authenticated by the account token the sandbox
    was given when the agent created it. Nothing is loaded by URL: World Clock appears in Plugins → Registry, listed
    only to this identity, and the reader installs it there. The template seeds the plugin's `PLUGIN.mdl`, the
    bundle ships it, and the registry opens it as the plugin's specification, so the spec round-trips from the
    project to the registry. Walked by the autocue flow
    `packages/plugins/plugin-projects/autocue/composer-plugin-registry.mjs`.
  actors: both
  status: verified
  given:
    - an identity exists and is associated with an account on the EDGE it talks to; on dev or preview a `test+…@dxos.org` email
      binds one with no invitation (the flow does this off camera)
    - space: a space is open (the default "My Space" is fine)
    - Composer is a production bundle (remote plugins install only from a bundle), built from a commit of `main` that
      pkg.pr.new has published or with `DX_PLUGIN_TOOLCHAIN_COMMIT` naming one, and with a `dx` that has
      `registry publish --private` (`DX_PLUGIN_TOOLCHAIN_CLI` until a release carries it)
    - EDGE runs registry-service with its `PLUGIN_REGISTRY_DB`, and the Assistant's model is one its ai-service serves
    - no plugin named "World Clock" is installed, and no "World Clock" project is left from an earlier run
  steps:
    - name: Create a project from the Composer Plugin template
      do: Click + on the space, choose Project, pick the "Composer Plugin" template, title it "World Clock" and click Create.
      capture: project
      expect: a "World Clock" project opens; its Tasks tab holds "Build the World Clock plugin" over four subtasks
    - name: Delegate the parent task to the agent
      do: Open the project's Tasks tab, tick the parent task, click "Delegate selected tasks to agent", then open the companion's Assistant tab.
      expect: the delegated chat holds the parent and its four subtasks and is working
    - name: Wait for the agent to publish the plugin
      do: Watch the subtasks tick off.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.queryObjects { typename: "org.dxos.type.sandbox" }
      capture: sandboxes
      expect: |
        a sandbox exists whose credentials export DX_API_TOKEN, all five tasks are finished, and the chat shows an
        `Uploaded: <edge>/registry/private/modules/…/manifest.json` line
      assert: return $result.results.length === 1
      note: |
        Creating the sandbox mints the token; no one grants it by hand. The template's publish step only asks for
        "Grant account access" when the token is missing. The delegated brief asks for sign-off, so the agent may
        leave the tasks in `review` rather than `done`.
    - name: Install World Clock from the registry
      do: Open Plugins, then Registry, find the World Clock card and click Install.
      expect: World Clock is listed without being loaded by URL, and installing it enables and activates it
    - name: Open the Clocks page and add two timezones
      do: Click Clocks under the WORLD CLOCK group, then add two timezones with the plus card.
      expect: the page shows the map above three ticking clock cards and the plus card
    - name: Open World Clock's specification from the registry
      do: Open Plugins → Registry, click the World Clock card, then click Open Specification.
      expect: |
        the spec opens beside the plugin with its definitions sidebar listing the `Zone` and `Clock` types, the
        `ClocksArticle` component and feature F-1, and its text is the `PLUGIN.mdl` the template seeded
        (`worldClockSpec`, with the key the project was given)
      note: |
        The spec reaches the registry only through the published bundle: `dx.config.ts` names it with `spec`, and the
        plugin contributes its text as a `pluginAsset`. Open Specification is absent when either is missing.
```

```mdl
test QA-3: Read a project's tasks as details
  desc: |
    A project's task list opens each task as the project plank's detail. With the deck flattened (the default)
    the task shows in a companion tab beside the project. Clicking an attachment card in the task moves the task
    into the main plank and shows the file beside it, and the breadcrumb goes back.
  actors: both
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists, with a space open
    - the deck is flattened (Settings → Deck), the default
    - a "Roastery Ops" project whose Tasks tab holds "Write a maintenance checklist for both roasters", with an
      image attached, and "Hire a part-time roastery assistant" (the flow's setup step creates them)
  steps:
    - name: Open the project's tasks
      do: Open the "Roastery Ops" project and its Tasks tab.
      expect: the project fills the main plank and nothing is open beside it
    - name: Open a task
      do: Click "Write a maintenance checklist for both roasters".
      expect: the task opens in a "Task" tab beside the project, and the project stays in the main plank
    - name: Open the task's attachment
      do: Click the attachment's card in the task.
      expect: |
        the task moves into the main plank with "Roastery Ops" in its breadcrumb, and the image opens in a "File"
        tab beside it
    - name: Go back to the project
      do: Click "Roastery Ops" in the breadcrumb.
      expect: the project is in the main plank again and the task is back in the "Task" tab beside it
    - name: Open another task
      do: Click "Hire a part-time roastery assistant".
      expect: it replaces the first task in the "Task" tab, and the first task and its attachment are closed
    - name: Open a task in its own plank
      do: Cmd-click "Write a maintenance checklist for both roasters".
      expect: |
        the task opens in the main plank with "Roastery Ops" in its breadcrumb, and its attachment is not open
        beside it
```

```mdl
test QA-4: Plan a project and hand its tasks to an agent
  desc: |
    The demo path — create a project from the Default template, brief its agent, break the work into tasks, fill
    one in, assign one from its row, let a coding session take one, and select tasks for the agent. Walked by the autocue flow
    `packages/plugins/plugin-projects/autocue/plan-a-project.mjs`; it stops short of delegating, so no model is
    needed.
  actors: human
  status: passing            # 2026-11-02, recorded by autocue/plan-a-project.mjs
  given:
    - an identity exists, on a fresh profile
    - space: the default space is open on its Home
  steps:
    - name: Create a project
      do: Click "Add to space", choose Project, title it "Roastery Ops", pick the Default template and click Save.
      capture: project
      expect: the project opens on its Overview tab, with the default agent instructions, and joins the navtree
        under Projects
    - name: Brief the project's agent
      do: Type a description, then add a line to the Instructions.
      expect: both fields keep what was typed
    - name: Break the work into tasks
      do: Open the Tasks tab and add "Service both roasters", "Order green beans" and "Hire a roastery assistant"
        from the Add task row, pressing Enter after each. Before adding the last, set its priority to Low from the
        row's Priority tag.
      expect: |
        the three tasks list in the order added. Each row shows its priority beside its status, ahead of the title,
        and ends in an assignee placeholder; "Hire a roastery assistant" shows Low, and the Priority tag reads unset
        again once it is added
    - name: Fill in a task
      do: Click "Service both roasters", type a description, set its priority to High and its status to Started.
      expect: |
        the task opens in a "Task" tab beside the project, and its Activity records the priority and status
        changes. Its row in the list shows the title only, not the description
    - name: Assign a task from its row
      do: Point at "Order green beans", click the assignee placeholder at the end of its row, and pick yourself.
      expect: |
        the picker lists the people, then a separator and the agents. The row shows your avatar in place of the
        placeholder, and the task does not open
    - name: A coding session takes a task
      do: |
        From a coding session connected over MCP, claim "Hire a roastery assistant" with `tasks-update`, passing
        status Started, `remoteSession` with the session's id, and the person `whoami` names as `assignee`.
      invoke: |
        org.dxos.operation.tasks.update {
          task: <"Hire a roastery assistant">, status: "started",
          assignee: { identityDid: $whoami.identityDid, name: $whoami.displayName },
          remoteSession: { sessionId: "qa-4", title: "Hire a roastery assistant" }
        }
      expect: |
        the row shows you as its assignee with the agent badge on your avatar, and its status is Started. Opening the
        task shows one Assignee row naming you, delegated to Claude Code: the agent, not the session's title
      note: Without `assignee` the claim is refused, since no person would answer for the task.
    - name: Select tasks for the agent
      do: |
        Tick "Order green beans", then tick "Hire a roastery assistant", then point at "Delegate selected tasks to
        agent".
      expect: |
        every row shows its checkbox, beside its mnemonic button. The two rows are selected and the button is enabled
      note: Clicking it starts a delegated chat that needs a model; QA-1 and QA-2 walk that path.
```

```mdl
test QA-5: Showcase clip
  desc: |
    The 20s showcase clip: Open a task of the Chess MCP on Workers project, then tick it and send it to the agent. Recorded light at 60 fps with no ident, captions or
    count-in by `tools/autocue/showcase/overnight.mjs`; the clip is the `clip: projects` step.
  actors: human
  status: unverified        # driven by autocue/showcase.mjs
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - plugin-debug, plugin-projects enabled
  steps:
    - name: Create the space from its template
      do: Enable plugin-debug, create the Chess MCP on Workers space, and open its project on Tasks.
      expect: the task tree shows
    - name: Open a task
      do: Click the first task.
      expect: the task's properties open
    - name: Send it to the agent
      do: Tick the first task and click Send to agent.
      expect: the Gantt panel opens
```

```mdl
test QA-6: Read a spec in its companion tabs
  desc: |
    A project's Specs tab shows a spec's text alone, and its other views are in three companion tabs: Structure,
    Decisions and Review. The spec is plugin-code's, whose types, components and decisions fill every view. Walked by
    the autocue flow `packages/plugins/plugin-projects/autocue/spec-companion.mjs`.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-4.7, F-4.8]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists, with a space open
    - plugin-code and plugin-projects enabled
    - a project "Code plugin" owns one spec, "CodePlugin", whose text is plugin-code's PLUGIN.mdl without its front
      matter
  steps:
    - name: Open the Specs tab
      do: Open the project "Code plugin" on its Specs tab.
      expect: the tab shows the spec's text and no view toggle, and the toolbar holds the spec dropdown and Extensions
    - name: Open the Structure tab
      do: Open the companion and its Structure tab.
      expect: the companion's tabs include Structure, Decisions and Review, and Structure draws the spec's definitions
        with a toggle of Definitions, Data model and API
    - name: Switch the Structure views
      do: Click Data model, then API.
      expect: each draws in the tab, and the spec's text stays in the main area
    - name: Give the spec the software package
      do: Open Extensions on the Specs tab and check Software.
      expect: the Structure toggle offers Canvas and Board after API
    - name: Show the canvas and the board
      do: Click Canvas, then Board.
      expect: each draws in the Structure tab
    - name: Open the Decisions tab
      do: Click the Decisions tab, then Decisions in its toggle.
      expect: the tab lists the spec's changelog and then draws its decisions
    - name: Show a decision from the margin
      do: Click the first decision chip in the margin.
      expect: the Decisions tab shows that decision selected
    - name: Open the Review tab
      do: Click the Review tab.
      expect: the tab says the spec has no review yet, with a toggle of Review and Code
```

```mdl
test QA-9: Two peers edit a project's notes
  desc: |
    Two peers already sharing a space open the same project, and each sees the other's caret in the overview's Notes,
    with the other's name. Automated at the end of the document-caret test, which shares its invitation and covers
    the late joiner.
  actors: human
  status: unverified
  automated:
    - composer-e2e:collaboration.spec.ts#Collaboration tests › host and guest can see each others' cursors when same document is in focus
  given:
    - host: a booted app with an identity, a space shared with the guest, and a project "Launch plan" in it
    - guest: a second browser profile with its own identity, joined to the host's space
  steps:
    - name: Open the project on the host
      do: Open "Launch plan" on the host.
      expect: the host shows the project's Notes
    - name: Open the project on the guest
      do: Open "Launch plan" on the guest.
      expect: the guest shows the project's Notes
    - name: Focus the notes on the host
      do: Put the cursor in the host's Notes.
      expect: the guest's Notes shows the host's caret with the host's name
    - name: Focus the notes on the guest
      do: Put the cursor in the guest's Notes.
      expect: the host's Notes shows the guest's caret with the guest's name
```

```mdl
test QA-10: Two peers see each other's carets in every project editor
  desc: |
    Alice and Bob share a project and each sees the other's named caret in the project's name and description, the
    overview's Notes, the agent's instructions, the spec's prose, and a feature's name, description and requirement
    inside the spec. Walked by the autocue flow `packages/plugins/plugin-projects/autocue/project-cursors.mjs`,
    recorded side by side.
  actors: human
  status: passing            # 2026-11-05, recorded by autocue/project-cursors.mjs
  given:
    - alice: a booted app named "Alice", with plugin-code on and a space holding a "Launch plan" project whose spec has
      prose and one feature, "Landing page", with a description and one requirement
    - bob: a second browser profile named "Bob", with plugin-code on, joined to Alice's space with "Launch plan" open
  steps:
    - name: Project name and description
      do: Bob types in the project's description, then Alice puts her cursor at the end of its name.
      expect: Alice's description shows Bob's caret and Bob's name shows Alice's, each with that peer's name
    - name: Notes
      do: Alice types in the overview's Notes, then Bob puts his cursor there.
      expect: each Notes shows the other peer's caret with that peer's name
    - name: Agent instructions
      do: Both open Config > Agent; Bob types in the instructions, then Alice puts her cursor there.
      expect: each instructions editor shows the other peer's caret with that peer's name
    - name: Spec prose
      do: Both open the Specs tab; Alice puts her cursor at the end of the prose, then Bob types after it.
      expect: each spec shows the other peer's caret with that peer's name
    - name: Feature name and description
      do: Bob puts his cursor at the end of "Landing page"'s name, then Alice types at the end of its description.
      expect: Alice's feature name shows Bob's caret and Bob's description shows Alice's, each with that peer's name
    - name: Feature requirement
      do: Alice types at the end of the feature's requirement, then Bob puts his cursor there.
      expect: each requirement row shows the other peer's caret with that peer's name
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: Features live on specs, not projects: a spec owns its features and its canvas layout, a feature nests by owning one spec, and a spec may name the project that tracks its tasks and hold one spec document.
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [QA-6]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: A showcase is a presenter slide deck, not a type of its own. Creating one makes a `Presentation` whose first slide is the write-up and whose further slides each show one artifact, such as an image or video file, a diagram or a pull request. The same `Demonstrates` relation links the deck to the features and tasks it shows.
  date: 2026-10-28
  by: user:dmaretskyi
```

```mdl
decision D-3: `Demonstrates` 0.2.0 takes any object as its source, and plugin-presenter implements `CreateShowcase` and migrates each `Showcase` 0.1.0 into a `Presentation` in place, keeping its id and its `Demonstrates` edges. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-10-28
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  why: plugin-presenter depends on this plugin through plugin-studio, so this plugin cannot import the `Presentation` type without a dependency cycle.
  rejected:
    - Moving `Presentation` into a package both plugins import, which moves the deck's whole type module for one relation.
```

```mdl
decision D-4: The Composer Plugin template's World Clock carries a spec that round-trips: the project that builds the plugin starts from it, and after the plugin is published to the private registry and installed, the registry's Open Specification shows it. QA-2 walks the round trip.
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [QA-2]
```

```mdl
decision D-5: The template seeds the spec's text and the agent writes it unchanged, rather than writing its own. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [QA-2]
  why: A fixed text makes the round trip checkable, since the registry must show exactly what the project started from.
  rejected:
    - The agent writes the spec from the task text, which differs on every run, so nothing can be compared.
```

```mdl
decision D-6: World Clock is published under the key `org.example.plugin.worldClock`, which the spec's `id` names. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [QA-2]
  why: The registry attaches a spec to the plugin whose key matches the asset's `pluginId`, and the spec names that key.
```

```mdl
decision D-7: Each project from the template gets its own plugin key, `org.example.plugin.worldClock` and six random hex digits, which the task text and the spec's `id` both carry. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [QA-2]
  supersedes: D-6
  why: Registry keys are global, so the fixed key was claimed by its first publisher and refused to every later one as "owned by another identity".
  rejected:
    - Let the agent pick a key: the spec's `id` would then differ from the text the template seeded.
```

```mdl
decision D-8: The minted key's suffix is eight random hex digits rather than six. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [QA-2]
  supersedes: D-7
  why: Six digits give 16.8 million keys, so a thousand projects collide with about a 3% chance, and a collision refuses the later publisher.
```

```mdl
decision D-9: A project owns its spec: `Project.spec` is a ref that makes the project the spec's ECHO parent, and the spec no longer names the project. A project has at most one spec, and a breakdown spec belongs to its feature, so no project can own it. Replaces the part of [D-1] where a spec names its project.
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [QA-6]
  supersedes: D-1
  why: Deleting a project takes its spec with it, and a project reads its spec directly instead of querying for the spec that names it.
```

```mdl
decision D-10: A project article shows one level of the project's spec: the project's own, or the breakdown of a feature beneath it. Every level is a nav-tree node under the project's Spec branch, addressed by the lineage of features above it, and opens the same article with Overview, Spec and Tasks narrowed to that level. A level's Spec tab shows only its own spec, never a slice of its parent's, and offers Add spec while there is none. The map's controls sit in the article's toolbar, and its views are Canvas, Kanban and Document, the last offering to write the spec's document while it has none.
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  why: Three stacked toolbars (the project's, the map's and its trail) left little room for the map. A level as a node puts the trail in the plank heading, keeps the selected tab as the reader goes down, and makes a feature deep in the map feel like part of the project.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: The Spec branch only groups the feature levels and is not selectable; the project's own spec is the project node's Spec tab. The map's views (Document, Canvas, Kanban, in that order) sit centred in the article's toolbar, and Add new feature and Add existing feature sit in its overflow, grouped above Create artifact. The Document view renders the spec's document as a section, which this plugin contributes as a plain MDL editor, so the view never depends on another plugin; it is also the article for a document no richer plugin renders. Refines [D-10].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  why: The spec is a part of the project, not a page of its own, and the document is the spec, with the map and board drawn from it. Adding features is occasional next to reading them.
```

```mdl
decision D-12: Every spec holds its document: `Spec` 0.2.0 makes `document` required, a new spec is made with one headed by its name, and the 0.1.0 migration gives each spec without one a new document. The Spec tab opens on the Document view. Importing a `.mdl` file into a spec writes the spec's own document and moves the file's path to it, unless the spec lies beneath the one already holding that path. Refines [D-1] and [D-11].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  why: The document is the spec; a spec without one has nothing to read, and the map and board are drawn from it.
```

```mdl
decision D-13: A feature level's nav-tree node holds the feature itself, so it gets every companion an object gets, such as the assistant, properties and debug. The project is found from the feature, through the specs above it. The article for a feature that a project tracks is that project's article narrowed to the feature; a feature no project tracks keeps its own article. The Spec branch holds no object, so it stays a group that opens nothing, and the deck leaves it out of a level's breadcrumbs. Refines [D-10] and [D-11].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  why: A feature is an object, so its level should behave like one; a level that wrapped the feature lost the assistant, properties and debug companions.

decision D-14: A feature's assistant chat runs in its project's context. The chat binds the spec that lists the feature and the project that tracks it, along with the project's skills, context objects and instructions. A feature no project tracks binds only its spec.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  why: A question about a feature is usually a question about its spec and project, so the assistant should not have to be told where the feature lives.
```

```mdl
decision D-15: A spec's document is where its features are kept. In ECHO the document holds an embed of each feature object, never a `feat` block, and the objects hold what each feature says. Importing a file maps each `feat` block into a feature object and puts the object's embed in place of the block. An embed is a block the editor does not let the reader enter, so its id cannot be corrupted, but it can be moved, reordered, copied and pasted. Features are added from the canvas or the board, or with a `/feature` command in the editor.
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1, F-2, F-3]
  why: The document, the map and the board are views of the same features, so they must agree with each other rather than refer to one another.
  rejected:
    - Keeping the `feat` blocks in the document beside the objects, which then drift apart.
```

```mdl
decision D-16: A spec's document enforces no structure. A spec may start as a wall of prose and gain structure over time; helpers such as slash commands, and later an auto-format, offer structure without requiring it. Refines [D-15].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.3, F-2.6, QA-7]
```

```mdl
decision D-17: `Spec.features` stays. The spec is each feature's ECHO parent, so deleting a spec deletes its features, and a parent needs a ref; the text could count as one in principle, but it is too loose. Refines [D-15].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-18: An exported `feat` block states the feature's status in a `status:` line. Deleting a feature's embed deletes the feature, and undo restores it. `DependsOn` stays an ECHO relation for now. Refines [D-15].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.3, F-3.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-19: plugin-code's two-way file sync writes a spec's document through export and reads its file through import, so the file in the repository keeps its `feat` blocks. plugin-code's spec article draws the feature cards as well, through a capability this plugin contributes. Refines [D-15].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.8, F-3.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-20: The document's embeds decide the spec's features. An embedded feature on no spec, deleted, or on a spec whose document no longer embeds it is claimed; one whose own spec's document still embeds it is a reference. A feature whose embed an edit took out moves to another spec whose document embeds it, or is deleted. A pasted embed of a feature the document already embeds becomes a copy. A feature the spec holds that its document never embedded is embedded at the end of its `## Features` section, or of the document. Fills in [D-15] and [D-17].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.4, F-1.5, F-2.4, F-2.5]
  why: A cut and a paste across two documents arrive as two separate edits in either order, so a removal moves the feature when another document embeds it. Seeds, migrations and other plugins add features by ref alone, so dropping what the text does not name would delete them.
  rejected:
    - Removing from the spec every feature its text does not embed.
```

```mdl
decision D-21: Importing a file takes a feature's name and description from it, as well as its requirements and status. Exporting writes against the file text last synced: the block of a feature that has not changed is copied as it was, a changed one has only its changed lines rewritten, and a block with no `status:` line reads as planned. Fills in [D-19].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.1, F-3.4]
  why: With the sync two-way, edits made in the app reach the file, so the file is current whenever it is read. Reprinting every block would flatten `when:`/`then:` requirements and wrapped lines across the repository on the first push.
  rejected:
    - Keeping a hand-edited name or description over the file's on import, which a pull would then push back over the file.
```

```mdl
decision D-22: `Feature` 0.4.0 records where a feature was read in from as foreign keys, source `github.com/dxos/dxos` and id `path#F-n`, in place of its `specs` field. The migrations from 0.2.0 and 0.3.0 move the field and put the feature's embed in place of its `feat` block, or at the end of its spec's document when the block is gone. Fills in [D-15].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.1]
  why: Import finds the feature a block was read in from by a foreign-key query, wherever the feature has moved since.
```

```mdl
decision D-23: This plugin defines and contributes `ProjectCapabilities.SpecDocumentCodec`, at startup, and `ProjectCapabilities.SpecDocumentExtensions`, a factory of editor extensions rather than a hook, once the app is idle; plugin-code consumes both. Fills in [D-19].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.8, F-3.5]
  why: plugin-code already depends on this plugin, which owns specs and their documents (org.dxos.plugin.code D-35). A sync pass before the codec arrives would read the file's `feat` blocks into the document as text. A factory can arrive after an editor opens, which rebuilds it, where a hook that arrives late breaks React's hook order.
```

```mdl
decision D-24: A feature embedded in a spec's document is drawn as an editable section of the feature, and its card is the read-only version. The section shows the feature's form, so it can be edited in place, as if the document were all Markdown. An embed is deleted the way other embeds are: Backspace with the cursor after it. Refines [D-15] and [D-19].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.1, F-2.3, F-2.7, F-2.9]
```

```mdl
decision D-25: The section holds the feature's core fields: name, description, requirements and status; subfeatures, dependencies and tasks stay in the companion. It is inert until it has attention, like other section embeds. After `/feature` the new section takes the cursor so it can be filled in place, and the companion does not open. Fills in [D-24].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.6, F-2.7]
```

```mdl
decision D-26: `/feature` after a sentence (text ending in `.`, `!` or `?`) makes the sentence the new feature's description and puts the cursor in its name; after a phrase, the phrase is the name and the cursor goes to the description. Fills in [D-25].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.6]
  why: A paragraph of prose is what a feature describes, not its name; making it the description is what turns unstructured text into a structured feature, and the name is then the one field left to write.
  rejected:
    - Always naming the feature by the line, which names a feature after a whole paragraph.
```

```mdl
decision D-27: A feature contributes a `section` surface holding its form, and embeds in a spec's document draw it inside the same inert-until-attended frame as other section embeds, with Escape returning the cursor to the text. Fills in [D-24] and [D-25].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.7, F-2.9]
  why: One form serves the spec's document, plugin-code's spec page and any Markdown document that embeds a feature; the companion's detail shares the same fields, so the two cannot drift.
```

```mdl
decision D-28: A spec file synced from a GitHub repository is read into features embedded in its document, as a file synced from a local checkout is. Refines [D-19].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-29: The codec makes a new spec of a document no object owns when a file read into it holds a `feat` block, and leaves a document of prose alone. The codec is `SpecText.codec`, which the capability contributes. Fills in [D-28].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.6]
  why: Both syncs make a bare document for a file they find, with nothing that owns it; the codec only handles a spec's document, so without this a file's blocks would stay text. A file with no `feat` block has nothing to be a spec of.
  rejected:
    - Making plugin-code create the spec, which would put plugin-projects' notion of a spec into plugin-code.
```

```mdl
decision D-30: A feature's section shows everything the feature's spec block would show if it were written in the document, including the features it depends on.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.10]
```

```mdl
decision D-31: The section shows the block id above the form, and lists the features the feature depends on with the same add and remove actions as its article. Dependents, tasks and showcases stay in the article and the companion. Fills in [D-30].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.10]
  why: The block id and the dependencies are what the request names that the form lacked; the other relations are not part of the block.
  rejected:
    - Embedding the whole article, which adds tasks, showcases and code links to every section in the document.
```

```mdl
decision D-32: Import reads a `desc:` field as the description and keeps each field of a structured requirement (`when:`, `then:`, `errors:`) on a line of its own; export writes the description as a `desc:` field. A fence that holds several `feat` blocks is split into one fence per block. Fills in [D-30].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.10, F-3.1, F-3.3, F-3.7]
  why: The section showed what import read, and import read a `desc:` field as prose starting with "desc:", joined a structured requirement's fields into one run-on line, and read only the first block of a fence, folding the rest into its last requirement.
  rejected:
    - Embedding a grouped fence's blocks inside the fence, where the editor does not draw embeds.
```

```mdl
decision D-33: The features a feature depends on stay `DependsOn` relations and are not written into its `feat` block, which has no field for them. Fills in [D-30].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.10, F-3.3]
  why: The `feat` dialect defines `desc` and `req` only, so a written `depends:` line would be outside the language every other spec reader uses.
  rejected:
    - Adding a `depends:` field to the `feat` dialect in this change.
```

```mdl
decision D-34: QA-7 ends by making "Instant inbox" depend on "Search" from its section. Fills in [D-31].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [QA-7, F-2.10]
  why: The walk already has two features in one document, so a dependency between them is one more step, and it is the part of the section the earlier steps never reach.
```

```mdl
decision D-35: Approve [D-33]: a feature's dependencies stay in ECHO only. An embed puts the structured ECHO object in the text in place of its fields as unstructured text, so the text duplicates none of its data.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.10, F-3.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-36: Every feature and requirement in a spec is numbered in the app, as it is in the `.mdl` text, not only those synced from a file or added by a seed. A spec in Composer is the MDL text made structured and must stay fully compatible with it. Numbers might follow the features' positions and need not be stored, though they may need to be.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.6, F-2.10]
```

```mdl
decision D-37: Ids are stored and never renumbered. A feature made in the app takes the next `F-n` after the highest in its spec when the spec is synced, and a requirement added to it takes the next id under the feature. A feature or requirement that moves or goes leaves the others' ids as they were. Fills in [D-36].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.6, QA-7]
  why: Specs, decisions and `@feature` tags in code cite features and requirements by id, so an id must name the same thing after a reorder. The `feat` dialect stores ids in the text for the same reason.
  rejected:
    - Numbering by position, which renumbers every feature after one that moves and breaks what cites them.
```

```mdl
decision D-38: An id the app gives a feature names no file until import reads its block in from one; import then maps that block to the feature numbered with its id. Fills in [D-37].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.1, F-3.2]
  why: An id recorded with the file's path would mark the feature as read in from the file, so importing the file before its block was written out would delete a feature added in the app. An id never written back left a file pulled after an export with a block no feature matched, which import then made a second feature of.
```

```mdl
decision D-39: Import reads a block without a `status:` line as planned, as export does, so a file that drops the line sets the feature to planned. Supersedes the import half of [D-21].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.1]
  why: Import kept the feature's status when the line was gone, and export, reading the missing line as planned, then wrote the old status back, so the file and the feature never agreed.
  rejected:
    - Export omitting `status:` only for planned features while import keeps the old status, which leaves the same loop.
```

```mdl
decision D-40: A `desc:` block scalar (`desc: |` or `desc: >`) is read as its prose, without the indicator. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.1]
  why: Repository specs such as org.dxos.plugin.code's F-14 write long descriptions as block scalars, and the indicator was read in as the first character of the description.
```

```mdl
decision D-41: `/feature` is offered only for a slash at the start of a line or after a space. Fills in [D-25].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.6]
  why: A slash inside a path such as `packages/` offered the completion, and Enter at the end of the line then accepted it, turning the paragraph into a feature.
```

```mdl
decision D-42: A feature holds one block of a file. When two blocks of a file name the same feature, the first keeps it and drops its other sources in that file, and each later block becomes a new feature. Fills in [D-15].
  date: 2026-11-02
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.1]
  why: A feature migrated from 0.3.0 can record two blocks of one file. Both blocks mapped to it, the later one's fields overwrote the earlier one's, and export wrote only the first embed back, losing the other block's text.
  rejected:
    - Merging the two blocks into one feature, which loses one block's name and requirements on export.
```

```mdl
decision D-43: A spec is one document. The canvas, the board and any other view are lenses over it, such as canvas positions or a kanban pivot, and the view toggle shows only when a spec has more than one view.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-4.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-44: Each dialect is one bundle, and the blocks it defines map to plugin capabilities when the text is synced. Composer's editor stays fully compatible with MDL as plain text.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-4.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-45: What a dialect adds to a spec is kept as ECHO annotations on the spec, so it is strongly typed.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-4.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-46: A spec declares its dialects as structured data, not in its text, and the sync to the text form uses them. A presentation dialect's blocks are inserted as templates by slash commands such as `/slide`, with no views of their own.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-4.3]
  why: The dialects of coding and of presentation are not mixed in one spec.
```

```mdl
decision D-47: A project has several top-level specs. The project article's Specs tab picks the current spec from a dropdown in its toolbar. Only feature specs nest, so a presentation spec is a top-level spec. Replaces the part of [D-9] where a project has at most one spec.
